WEBVTT

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.260
Why...

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.640
Oh, Oikawa.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.320
You came just in time.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.530
Looks like there's no way to tell what 
it is if we can't get in from here.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.530
You believe me?

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.800
Just come over here, Leader.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.910
We didn't know about 
that opening, either.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.210
We'll never get anywhere without you.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.470
I wonder if there's some trick

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.170
to this wall, like those puzzle boxes?

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.590
I don't know, but usually,

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.920
these things are simple 
once you get them open.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.520
...you believe me?

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.030
Whoa, what are you doing?!

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.740
How annoying, get off of me!

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:38.030
Suddenly I'm starving!

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.330
Let's go eat, boys!

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.140
Shut up!

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:45.750
This is the son

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.540
of a family who runs a business 
producing mold-starters,

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.490
who started attending agricultural 
university this spring,

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.690
Sawaki Souemon Tadayasu-kun.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:55.720
He has the ability to see 
microbes with the naked eye.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:56.980
Amazing, isn't it?

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.340
Oikawa doesn't know 
about it yet, though.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.990
Really? You're keeping it a secret?

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.460
Moyashimon

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:09.460
Returns

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.700
<i>You said my marriage could wait</i>

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.720
<i>until I gain some results 
from my research.</i>

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:44.630
<i>Then, if I may ask,</i>

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.220
<i>when are these results going to show up?</i>

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.010
<i>But a promise is a promise.</i>

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.040
<i>I have no intention of 
quitting school just yet.</i>

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.280
<i>Then I have an idea as well.</i>

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.470
This isn't good.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.990
Hasegawa-san

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:08.010
Please leave a message after the tone.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.980
Hasegawa-san, please get here soon!

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.840
There's something we want to ask you!

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:16.800
We're being forced to 
work for the soybean team

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.340
even though we're on the rice team!

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.340
An opening?

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.820
I don't know anything 
about that. What opening?

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.220
Hey! Don't stop working!

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.050
You think it's a secret lab of Itsuki's 
or something, right, Oikawa?

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:37.860
I think Yuuki-kun knows something,

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.610
about what's underground, 
if not about the opening.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.110
Hiyoshi Liquor Store

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:49.870
And I get the feeling Hasegawa-san 
knows something, too.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.800
Speaking of Haruka-san, 
she's not here yet.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.840
I left her a voicemail.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.490
She's coming, right?

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.900
I hope she gets here soon.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.450
They've got to be able to 
handle things themselves,

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.990
in case I get forced 
to go back someday.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.790
Hey, Misato-kun, you're empty-handed!

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:10.700
Carry those miso balls!

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.140
Going out for a smoke.

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:19.190
Fermenting Cellar

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:21.670
Sawaki will have to tell the

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:25.200
microbe killer the truth sooner or later.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.700
Oh well, I'm sure he can do it.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.700
They seem to get along well.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.710
Oh, yeah, I need to carry the 
miso balls... break's over...

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.410
I just came to see 
how things were going.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.100
Looks like you guys have 
things under control.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:52.980
I leave it in your hands.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.730
Hasegawa-san's still not here.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.480
She was here, but she left... I think.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:03.990
What?!

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:04.900
She was here?

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.620
I wanted to ask her about the opening.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.040
Haruka-san's probably pretty busy, too.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.430
Come on! Without Haruka-san,

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.570
we've gotta keep moving 
or it'll never get done!

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.170
I'll show you!

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:23.240
Oikawa's working hard!

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.290
Better work hard, too, Tadayasu!

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:26.050
Shut up.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:27.010
Grains (Rice This Time) + Malt

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:37.060
Wheat + Soybeans + Malt                               Grains (Rice This Time) + Malt

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:37.060
Gonna Burst!

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:37.060
Gonna Burst!

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:37.060
Soybeans

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:37.060
Soy Sauce Barrel                                                   Miso Barrel

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.150
Today in the fermenting cellar, 
they're making rice malt miso.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.660
The difference between 
this and making soy sauce

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:37.060
is when the soybeans and 
malt are mixed together.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:37.770
Malt Cellar 2 (Miso)

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.560
Malt Cellar 2 (Miso)

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.050
Cover it up, you moldy 
little chicken shits!

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:52.950
Steamed Rice

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:44.810
Sir, yes, sir!

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:44.830
Malt is poured over rice that's

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:46.840
been steamed in a steaming basket

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.510
to make rice malt.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.550
The amount of rice malt used

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.530
in proportion to the amount of soybeans

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:55.350
determines how sweet or 
sharp the miso will be.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.450
Steamed Rice

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:55.330
Steamed Rice

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.740
This time we're making a 
type called 100% malt,

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.060
a sweet, mild miso made 
with equal amounts of

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.140
soybeans and rice malt.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.830
The beans are soaked in water 
during preliminary preparations,

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.390
until they double in size.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:14.350
Occupied

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:11.610
Using an equal quantity of rice malt

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.350
means we would need 
twice as many soybeans.

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:14.770
Rice Cross-section

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:29.110
Rice Cross-section

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:29.110
 Gonna Burst!

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:17.250
Next we burst it, boys!

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.730
Sir, yes, sir!

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:23.130
Then, once we reach the 
rice, we "burst" it.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:26.030
That's what we call it when 
all of us microbes diffuse

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.110
through the rice, right to its 
center, turning it into rice malt.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.370
Burst

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.370
By the way, this is how it's written!

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.420
Meanwhile, the steamed 
and crushed soybeans

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:36.770
are formed into lumps 
called "miso balls,"

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.870
and allowed to sit for two 
weeks to dry the outsides.

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:43.060
Drying the outsides allows 
microbes that will help

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.600
in making the miso to 
take hold on the surface,

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.220
while lactic acid bacteria 
work on the insides,

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.610
giving the miso its acidic flavor.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.780
Once these preparations are done,

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:57.110
crushed miso balls, salt,

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:57.770
Salt

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:58.890
and rice malt are mixed together,

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.890
and it's finally time to make miso.

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:03.030
Oh, and making the rice malt is timed

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:06.430
to match the end of the two-week 
miso ball preparation.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:11.160
Yay!                Yay!               Yay!             Yay!             Yay!

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:11.160
Miso

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:11.160
This process is 
only one example.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.570
That's the quick version, 
but there you go -

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:11.160
that's how miso is made!

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:15.850
I didn't know you had to stimulate 
the fermentation of miso.

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.370
It takes a lot more work than soy sauce.

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:20.630
Your family owns a mold-starter 
business and you didn't know that?

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:22.040
And you call yourself Souemon?

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:26.420
I'm just the carefree second son...

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.420
There are still many miso 
producers that make miso

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:33.930
using the ancient Japanese 
miso ball technique.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:38.680
Incidentally, I hear Hiyoshi 
Liquor Store is reopening.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:41.190
Would anyone like to go visit with me?

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.090
Yes! Me! Me! I'll go!

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:45.940
Not until we're finished 
making the miso balls!

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.840
You can come after you finish.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:59.450
Yuuki-kun is at Hiyoshi Liquor Store.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.420
We might be able to ask 
him about the opening.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:04.560
Let's get to work, boys!

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.460
Yeah!

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:08.800
<i>I'm getting nowhere,</i>

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.470
<i>in my research, and everything else.</i>

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.470
<i>No matter where I try to run,</i>

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:17.400
<i>I'm still just a bird in a cage.</i>

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:21.020
Haruka seems down.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:23.490
I really wish she'd look at me again.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.480
Cheer up, Haruka!

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.480
Hiyoshi Liquor Store

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.730
Welcome to the new Hiyoshi Liquor Store!

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.490
This is what my ideal 
liquor store looks like.

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.930
I thought a store that didn't 
offer a view of the inside

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:38.660
might seem harder for people to enter.

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:40.110
I got rid of the curtain and shelves,

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.500
and widened the entrance.

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.750
Doesn't this big thing cover a lot?

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:48.770
That blocks direct sunlight during 
the brightest part of the day.

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.130
You didn't even know that?

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:51.760
Keep your mouth shut, idiot.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:56.010
Yuuki Liquor

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:54.930
Please come in.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.130
Wow!

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:01.520
It does look much bigger and brighter!

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.610
R-Really?

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:13.010
Hey there, kiddo.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:17.290
I thought a town liquor store 
should be more specialized.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:21.440
So we've limited or done away 
with beers sold by the case,

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:23.450
and we no longer sell big 
name sake or other liquors

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.410
that can be bought at supermarkets 
or convenience stores.

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.920
How long do you think this store 
has supplied those things?

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.790
But doesn't that mean you'll have

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:33.530
fewer students as customers?

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:36.030
Even though we're close to the school,

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.520
surprisingly few students come here.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.520
If students have money,

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:42.800
they'd rather use it to fill their 
bellies, not buy alcohol.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:44.810
Sorry, Sawaki!

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:48.210
Wh-Why are there so many microbes?

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:50.620
The brewery near here is remodeling.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:51.670
The wood they removed

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:53.820
had a good flavor because 
of the cellar yeast,

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.570
so we took it to use in 
the interior decorating.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:59.840
Cellar yeast refers to 
yeasts and other microbes

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:01.600
that reside on surfaces in breweries.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:03.370
Sawaki, are you okay?

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:05.580
I'll try to ignore it. 
Don't worry about me.

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:09.470
It is true that the town's liquor 
stores have a disadvantage.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:12.960
Supermarkets and discount stores 
sell alcohol at lower prices,

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.190
and convenience stores sell all

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:17.470
kinds of snacks to eat with the drinks.

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:19.080
That's right!

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:20.840
Competing head-to-head 
with them won't work!

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:24.970
That's why, instead of selling 
rare and premium liquors,

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.830
we want to use sake and 
 become the kind of store

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:30.400
that'll make people say, 
"You can find that at Hiyoshi."

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.140
There are many types of sake,

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.290
so just having lots of types that 
you don't see in bigger stores

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:37.900
gives them a sort of rare feel.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.170
We gave all the product 
labels a modern pop look.

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:43.520
We'll also share stories about 
the breweries they came from,

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.660
and offer samples when possible, too.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:46.950
Samples?!

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:48.740
People can taste the drinks?

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:50.330
We'll have to drop in more often.

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:53.500
The ultimate goal is to help customers

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.340
find their favorite bottle,

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:57.360
but first and foremost, 
I want this to be

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:59.090
a store people can easily walk into.

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:02.990
But that's no different

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:05.660
from the sake tasting corners with 
sommeliers at department stores.

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:07.260
It's not unusual at all.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:10.350
There are liquor stores all 
over town that offer samples.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:12.440
Oh, there are?

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:13.780
Then start doing the same.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:15.110
Do your research!

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:17.580
I didn't know! I'm a minor!

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:18.360
Yuuki Liquor

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:18.360
This Week's Owner's Recommendation

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.360
What?

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:23.240
This week, we'll be featuring 
my family's brewery

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:25.690
in "This Week's Owner's 
Recommendation."

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:28.290
Starting today, I'd like you to 
visit breweries all around Japan

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:31.120
and send back information 
on the good ones each week.

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:33.630
Be sure to talk to the 
workers in each brewery.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:35.690
If you hear any interesting info,

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:38.130
it will be easier for us 
to sell their products.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.130
I'll give you one year.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.100
Off you go!

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:42.890
What?!

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.890
I see. It is faster to 
learn through doing.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.210
Yuuki-kun, you've totally 
taken the lead in this store!

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:53.900
Kei, since when...

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.580
That may indeed be a good idea.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:58.900
If we kept running this store 
like before, it would never last.

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:01.890
And you always said you 
wanted to become a TV star,

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:03.910
but you've never done anything about it.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.380
A TV star?!

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:07.910
Don't tell them that, Grandpa...

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:10.900
You're working at this 
store, so get your attitude

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:12.310
in line and work!

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:14.450
But I'm still doing 
my skin care routine!

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:17.030
Want to ask Kei about 
the you-know-what?

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:19.030
No. I'll work it out myself.

00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:20.360
Oikawa!

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:22.930
Yourself? Aren't we a team?

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:26.430
I'm going to have a drink. 
You all head on back.

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:28.490
Oikawa!

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:39.610
Oikawa!

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:41.800
It's Tadayasu!

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:42.650
It's Tadayasu!

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:46.030
Tadayasu, Haruka seems sort of down.

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:48.030
Hasegawa-san's here?

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.550
Do you know what's under this school,

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:58.280
or about any openings that lead there?

00:12:58.280 --> 00:12:58.920
Openings?

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.990
You don't know, either?

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:04.430
I've never heard of 
anything underground.

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:07.140
If Hasegawa-san doesn't even know,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:08.550
then maybe it really is a secret.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:11.590
I guess we'll have to ask the professor.

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:13.560
Hasegawa-san...

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:18.190
<i>What is the research that food 
researchers desire the most?</i>

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:21.370
The professor asked me that.

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:22.760
Hmm...

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.680
Assuming that, by what 
we want to do the most,

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:29.200
he meant something we 
can't actually do...

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:31.610
Thinking like the professor would,

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:32.950
I'd say experimenting on humans.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:42.250
The results you get from 
experimenting on mice

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:44.480
are only the results for the mice.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.550
So I'm just thinking in extremes, 
like the professor might.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:51.220
But if I had to give a reason for it,

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:52.710
experiments on mice are

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:56.730
conducted to test the safety 
or danger of products.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:58.650
Even if one product is safe,

00:13:58.650 --> 00:13:59.970
we don't know if it's still safe

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.480
when combined with something else.

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:06.670
Some time ago, it was found 
that mixing preservatives

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:10.240
in vitamin drinks produced toxins.

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:12.080
When you think of it that way,

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:14.020
for instance, some say that long-term

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:15.790
experiments are being conducted

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:20.210
to quietly test the effects of 
marketed food additives on humans.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:24.310
But I'm only thinking in 
extremes, so don't...

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:28.690
It's open.

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:30.370
I'm surprised...

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:32.170
I really didn't know this was here.

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:35.770
This saves us the trouble 
of breaking down the wall.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:39.820
What do you suppose this 
opening is, Oikawa?

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:42.900
Honestly, I have no idea,

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:44.530
but I was thinking Professor Itsuki

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:47.750
may be using an old 
underground military facility

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:51.030
to conduct some sort 
of scary experiment.

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:54.370
It wouldn't surprise me if he was.

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:55.470
Do you think that because

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:57.540
I was talking about human experiments?

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:00.430
There was nothing in the 
university records about

00:15:00.430 --> 00:15:02.860
the military using any facilities 
here as research labs.

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:05.800
Yet there were records of a parade 
ground and anti-aircraft artillery,

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:09.800
and we know the professor did 
research on aviation fuel.

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.140
So I thought he must have

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:15.010
done some other kind 
of research here, too.

00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:17.740
It does seem like there's a lot about

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:20.310
the professor's research 
that we don't know.

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:23.920
It wouldn't surprise me if there 
is something underground.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:24.950
What do you want to do?

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:27.690
Itsuki and or even Yuuki 
might be down there now.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:29.830
After all the investigating I've done

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.560
and hearing what Hasegawa-san said,

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:35.230
honestly, I'm wondering if I'd 
be better off not knowing.

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:38.880
But if I'm going to have regrets 
whether I find out or don't find out,

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:39.960
then I'd rather find out.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:42.020
I want to know.

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:52.970
It's just a crazy idea, but...

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:55.140
Remember when Aya-san was

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:57.470
talking about the legends of this school?

00:15:57.470 --> 00:16:01.290
Oh, like the stories about 
students suddenly disappearing,

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:03.480
and that thing about the lake?

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:05.030
Now that you mention it,

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:07.050
I heard the foundation of 
the windmill on the lake

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.990
is the same one remaining from a 
building the military once used.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:12.160
Oikawa said she found it 
in the university records.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:15.330
When you add that to the equation...

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:18.540
Why would you add something 
that stupid to the equation?

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:32.740
Yeast?

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:45.850
I see you're all together...

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:49.130
I guess I should have shut the door.

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:50.860
P-Professor...

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:52.990
What's down there?

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:57.760
Come on. I'll show you.

00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:10.120
The temperature is stable underground...

00:17:10.120 --> 00:17:12.670
So I made some wort last summer,

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:17.770
added wild yeast, and brought 
it down here to ferment.

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:19.790
In the case of top-fermented beer,

00:17:19.790 --> 00:17:21.820
the ideal temperature for 
the yeast to do its work

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:24.210
is between 10 and 25 degrees Celsius.

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:25.900
And since underground temperatures stay

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:28.150
in that range even throughout summer...

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:31.690
See? The beer's done, no problem.

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:34.470
Can we have some?

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.450
Certainly. But you'd drink it all, 
wouldn't you, Mutou-kun?

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:40.950
That's why I wanted to 
keep this place a secret.

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:43.260
But recently it's been 
getting cold at night,

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:44.960
so I guess you wouldn't 
drink lots of it.

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:46.890
I don't know about that...

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:48.990
Come on over, everyone.

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:52.960
Beer? That's it?

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:55.460
This is a malt cellar.

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:58.460
I'm thinking of preparing 
some sake here.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:17:59.850
A malt cellar?

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:02.470
Amazing, isn't it? I was surprised, too.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.360
But that's...

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:10.380
Professor, what exactly is 
this underground passage?

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:14.190
It was originally Hiyoshi Liquor's 
malt cellar and storeroom.

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:16.860
H-Hiyoshi Liquor?

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:20.440
The Hiyoshi Liquor Store 
used to be a brewery.

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:23.620
Hiyoshi Liquor Store First Generation

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:21.580
The grandfather closed the

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:23.620
brewery and started the liquor store.

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:28.510
Malt cellars and storehouses like 
this were often constructed

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:30.490
beneath breweries and fermenting facilities

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:32.630
built on good, solid ground.

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:36.660
You don't see them much anymore, though,

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:39.130
due to issues with 
property lines and such.

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:43.110
Fortunately, Hiyoshi's underground 
extended to this campus,

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:45.130
so it was able to remain here.

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:48.890
You mean this and all 
the other routes are...

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:51.680
The opening at Hiyoshi-san's 
end was just

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:53.740
connected to this one 
in its construction.

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:56.900
Though, this is the only 
route we can still use.

00:18:56.900 --> 00:19:00.490
Oh, but this route leads to Hiyoshi's

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:03.260
secret nighttime bar that 
you've all visited once before,

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:05.170
so you mustn't use it.

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:23.050
Everyone's drinking.

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:27.030
I'm hopeless, aren't I?

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:30.310
Getting all excited on my own...

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:33.030
And it turned out to be 
just a simple passageway.

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:36.360
Sawaki-kun, I apologize

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:38.940
for dragging you along and 
putting you through all that.

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:41.530
That's what was bothering you?

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:44.950
Hey, anyone would get curious if 
they saw an opening like that.

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:53.960
Oikawa... maybe I'm the 
one who should apologize.

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:00.020
There's something I've 
been hiding from you.

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:02.220
The professor and all the 
others know about it, too.

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:06.720
You're the only one I haven't told.

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:09.140
I'll tell you now.

00:20:09.610 --> 00:20:10.980
Come with me, Oikawa.

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Moyashimon

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Returns

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Episode 3

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.950
Microbe 
Theater

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:52.950
Microbe Theater Returns!

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:52.950
Returns

00:21:56.790 --> 00:21:59.670
It sure is hard for humans to make miso.

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:02.090
All they're doing is preparing it.

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:05.190
We microbes are the ones who 
actually make the miso.

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:06.760
That's true!

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:10.430
Miso itself has a very long history.

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:13.660
There are theories that it 
was brought over from China,

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:15.940
but records of life in the 
Jomon era have shown that

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:17.440
people back then had already

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:20.230
made something called 
"Jomon miso" from acorns.

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:23.970
Records of the Asuka period mention 
something called "mishou,"

00:22:21.570 --> 00:22:27.320
Mishou

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:27.320
the product that would come 
to be known as "miso."

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:31.240
It's said that the name "miso" was 
derived from this "mishou"...

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:32.910
Mishou

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:34.830
Miso

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:37.040
But the character used to 
write the "so" in "miso"

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:41.500
Miso

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:39.140
is not used anywhere else.

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:41.500
It was invented just for miso!

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:45.150
Expect no less of the 
fermented goods we brew!

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:48.020
We'll keep brewing up the 
good stuff from now on!
